---
// /proof — one site built with Webcroft, with everything that can be measured
// about it. Positioning term (tests/positioning.spec.ts): "built with Webcroft".
// Owner decision 2026-10-04: genai-wednesday.de is the one site shown here.
// Honesty rules (CONTENT_GUIDE.md): every figure is dated and names its tool; the
// search table is computed from the published file (src/data/proof.ts).
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config';
import { PROOF, PROOF_MEASURED, SITE_PROOF } from '../data/proof';

const p = SITE_PROOF;
const speed = PROOF.find((entry) => entry.site === p.site)!;
const rerun = `https://pagespeed.web.dev/analysis?url=${encodeURIComponent(p.url)}&form_factor=mobile`;
const { latest, peak, blocks } = p.search;
---
<Base
  title="Built with Webcroft: one site, measured"
  description="Built with Webcroft: genai-wednesday.de, with its PageSpeed scores, its Google clicks since the relaunch, and a check of which AI assistants name it."
  image="/images/og/proof.jpg"
>
  <section class="wrap hero">
    <h1>Built with Webcroft: one site, measured</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      <a href={p.url}>{p.site}</a> is the site Webcroft grew out of. This page shows
      what can be measured about it: speed, clicks from Google since its relaunch,
      and whether AI assistants name it. Every figure has a date. Where you can
      re-run a test, there is a link.
    </p>
    <p class="aside">
      It is one site, and it belongs to the maintainer of Webcroft. Read it as a
      worked example, not as a promise for yours.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="site">
    <h2>The site</h2>
    <p>
      {p.site} is the home of GenAI Wednesday, a generative AI community in Munich:
      events, an archive of talks and a set of resource pages. It was relaunched on
      <time datetime={p.relaunched.iso}>{p.relaunched.label}</time> with these
      skills. The copy of the skills and tests in its repository is stamped with
      toolkit commit <code>{p.toolkit.commit}</code>, taken on
      <time datetime={p.toolkit.copied.iso}>{p.toolkit.copied.label}</time>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="speed">
    <h2>Speed and search basics</h2>
    <dl class="scores">
      <div><dt>Performance</dt><dd>{speed.performance}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Accessibility</dt><dd>{speed.accessibility}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Best practices</dt><dd>{speed.bestPractices}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>SEO</dt><dd>{speed.seo}</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <p>
      Google's PageSpeed Insights, on a simulated phone, for the home page: one run
      on <time datetime={PROOF_MEASURED.iso}>{PROOF_MEASURED.label}</time>. Scores
      run from 0 to 100 and vary a little from run to run.
      <a href={rerun}>Re-run the test</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="search">
    <h2>Clicks from Google since the relaunch</h2>
    <p>
      In the 28 days from {latest.from} to {latest.to}, Google sent
      <strong>{latest.clicks} clicks</strong> to the site, from
      {latest.impressions.toLocaleString('en-GB')} appearances in search results.
      The table shows every 28-day block since the relaunch.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Clicks from Google in 28-day blocks" tabindex="0">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">28 days</th><th scope="col" class="num">Clicks</th><th scope="col" class="num">Appearances</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {blocks.map((b) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{b.from} to {b.to}</th>
              <td class="num">{b.clicks}</td>
              <td class="num">{b.impressions.toLocaleString('en-GB')}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Clicks rose from almost nothing to a best 28 days of {peak.clicks}
      ({peak.from} to {peak.to}), and have fallen since. The cause of the fall is
      not established.
    </p>
    <p>
      For its main local searches the site sits near the bottom of the first
      results page:
      {p.search.positions.map((q, i) => (
        <Fragment>{i > 0 && ', and '}<q>{q.query}</q> at an average position of {q.position}</Fragment>
      ))}, in the latest block.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      Source: the site's own Google Search Console account, whole site, all
      countries. You cannot re-run these figures, so the daily numbers behind them
      are published: <a href={p.search.csv}>download the file</a> (CSV). The latest
      block ends on the last day Search Console had data for when the file was made.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="ai">
    <h2>Do AI assistants name it?</h2>
    <p>
      On <time datetime={p.ai.date.iso}>{p.ai.date.label}</time> the weekly check
      asked each assistant two questions, up to three times each:
    </p>
    <ul class="steps">
      {p.ai.questions.map((question) => <li><q>{question}</q></li>)}
    </ul>
    <div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Results of the AI check" tabindex="0">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Assistant</th><th scope="col">With web search</th><th scope="col">Without web search</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {p.ai.rows.map((row) => (
            <tr><th scope="row">{row.assistant}</th><td>{row.withSearch}</td><td>{row.withoutSearch}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      When an assistant searches the web, it tends to find the site and name it.
      From memory alone, none did. The check counts mentions for two questions on
      one day, and answers change from run to run. It talks to each assistant
      through its API, which can answer differently from the app.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      This is a different thing from the panel in the
      <a href={p.lab}>Builder Lab</a>, where four assistants rate the site. A rating
      is an opinion; this check only counts whether the site is named.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" id="limits">
    <h2>What this page does not show</h2>
    <ul class="steps">
      <li><strong>More than one site.</strong> One example cannot show that a result repeats.</li>
      <li><strong>A comparison.</strong> Nothing here shows what the site would have done without Webcroft.</li>
      <li><strong>Large numbers.</strong> This is a community site with tens of clicks a month, not a traffic story.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The <a href={p.lab}>Builder Lab</a> keeps a fuller scorecard for the same site.
    </p>
    <div class="cta">
      <a class="btn primary" href={SITE.repo}>Get Webcroft on GitHub</a>
      <a class="btn" href="/#quickstart">Quickstart</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  section.wrap { padding: 2.5rem 1.25rem; max-width: var(--maxw); margin: 0 auto; }
  section.hero { padding-top: 3rem; }
  .hero h1 { font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3rem); line-height: 1.1; margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  h2 { margin-bottom: 0.8rem; line-height: 1.25; }
  section p, .steps { max-width: 46rem; line-height: 1.7; hyphens: manual; }
  .lead { font-size: 1.15rem; color: var(--muted); }
  .note { font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--muted); }
  code { font-size: 0.9em; }
  .steps { padding-left: 1.3rem; }
  .steps li { margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }

  .scores { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.8rem 2.5rem; margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  .scores div { display: flex; flex-direction: column-reverse; }
  .scores dt { font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--muted); }
  .scores dd { margin: 0; font-size: 2.2rem; font-weight: 700; line-height: 1.1; color: var(--pass); }

  .table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; margin: 1.2rem 0; max-width: 46rem; }
  table { border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; }
  th, td { text-align: left; vertical-align: top; padding: 0.6rem 0.9rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); line-height: 1.5; }
  thead th { font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--muted); }
  tbody th { font-weight: 400; }
  .num { text-align: right; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
  #search tbody tr:last-child th, #search tbody tr:last-child td { font-weight: 700; }
  #ai .table-wrap { max-width: none; }
  #ai tbody th { font-weight: 600; white-space: nowrap; }
</style>
